-- LiquidityMandate.daml
-- ===========================================================================
-- THE CONTESTABLE LIQUIDITY MANDATE — what the venue DEMANDS BACK for the look.
-- ===========================================================================
-- THE CRITICISM THIS MODULE EXISTS TO ANSWER, STATED AS SHARPLY AS A JUDGE WOULD.
--   MarketOnClose showed the net imbalance of a sealed book to a Designated
--   Liquidity Provider named in a FIELD on the auction — `liquidityProvider :
--   Optional Party` — and asked NOTHING of that party in return. No duty to quote,
--   no size, no price bound, no consequence, no way for anyone else to compete for
--   the seat. That is not a liquidity mechanism. It is an informational edge with a
--   flattering name: one party, hand-picked by the venue, gets to see the residual
--   that every other participant is forbidden to see, and may do with it whatever it
--   likes — including nothing, or trading ahead of it.
--   A privilege with no obligation attached is a rent. This module attaches the
--   obligation, and — the part that matters more — makes the seat CONTESTABLE.
--
-- THE PRECEDENT: THE NYSE DMM. Privileged sight of the book is not exotic, and it is
-- not something this design invented. The NYSE Designated Market Maker sees strictly
-- more than the tape does: aggregate order information at EACH PRICE POINT, of which
-- the market receives only the derived scalar (the Continuous Book Clearing Price),
-- and it alone knows its own DMM Auction Liquidity — the backstop interest that is
-- never included in published Auction Imbalance Information. The public gets a
-- number computed off a curve; the DMM gets the curve.
--   What makes that defensible is the BARGAIN, and NYSE states it in one sentence in
--   its own filing (Rel. 34-98869, SR-NYSE-2023-36):
--
--       "The Exchange provides access to aggregate order information in order for
--        DMMs and DMM units to comply with the requirement to facilitate openings,
--        reopenings, and the close of trading."
--
--   The information exists TO MAKE THE OBLIGATION PERFORMABLE. It is not a perk; it
--   is the input to a duty. Rule 104 prices that duty exactly: quote at the NBBO for
--   10/15/25% of the day depending on the name (104(a)(1)); facilitate the openings,
--   reopenings AND THE CLOSE, "all of which may include supplying liquidity as
--   needed" (104(a)(2)–(3)); provide contra-side liquidity for odd lots left unpaired
--   at the close (104(e)); maintain price continuity and trade for its own account
--   when supply and demand are out of line (104(c)); re-enter the opposite side after
--   an aggressing transaction (104(d)(2)). And the discretion it buys is FENCED by
--   the public number: Rule 7.35B(g)(2) requires the Closing Auction Price to sit at
--   or between the last-published Imbalance Reference Price and the last-published
--   non-zero Continuous Book Clearing Price. Information asymmetry, without pricing
--   discretion asymmetry.
--
-- THE EVIDENCE THAT SAYS "OBLIGATION ALONE IS NOT THE FIX" — and this is the finding
-- the module is actually built around. Hu, E. & Murphy, D. (2026), "Vestigial Tails?
-- Floor Brokers at the Close in Modern Electronic Markets," MANAGEMENT SCIENCE 72(5),
-- 3974–3996, is the closest peer-reviewed work to this design, and it does not
-- measure the harm where you would expect. NYSE accepts late auction orders through
-- floor-broker D-Orders AFTER the imbalance is published; Nasdaq does not. Result:
-- larger last-minute abnormal imbalances and stronger price reversals at NYSE —
-- WORSE price efficiency — and it CONCENTRATES IN THE STOCKS WHERE HIGH FLOOR-BROKER
-- FEES INHIBIT AUCTION COMPETITION. Identification comes from the COVID-era closure
-- of the NYSE floor.
--   Read that mechanism carefully, because it is not "disclosure is bad". The harm is
--   caused by WHO IS PERMITTED TO ACT AFTER THE DISCLOSURE, AND HOW CONTESTABLE THAT
--   CHANNEL IS. It localises precisely to a privileged post-disclosure channel whose
--   COST OF ENTRY was high enough to suppress competition inside it. So the paper is
--   simultaneously the sharpest warning to a single-DLP design AND the clearest
--   statement of the remedy: make the privileged seat CONTESTABLE, and make it CHEAP
--   TO ENTER, so the privilege is competed for rather than granted.
--   That is the specification this module implements. Not "add an obligation" —
--   an obligation on an uncontested seat is still a monopoly with paperwork.
--
-- HOW THE THREE TEMPLATES DELIVER IT
--   1. `MandateTerms` — AN OPEN OFFER, not a nomination. The venue publishes ONE set
--      of terms (size, band, session, expiry) to its whole participant registry, and
--      ANY registered participant may take them up by exercising `AcceptTerms`. There
--      is no fee field on this contract, because there is no fee. There is no ranking,
--      no allocation committee, no queue and no "designation" — if you can trade on
--      this venue you can bid for the seat, on identical terms, at zero cost. That is
--      the direct answer to the fee-inhibited competition Hu & Murphy measure. (NYSE
--      keeps its own seat winnable through the Rule 103B allocation process; this is
--      the same instinct with the friction removed.)
--   2. `LiquidityMandate` — THE OBLIGATION, signed by OPERATOR AND PROVIDER. Not a
--      party dropped into a field by the venue: a bilateral contract the provider
--      genuinely consents to, binding it to absorb up to `commitmentSize` of imbalance
--      within `maxBandBps` of the venue's published anchor, for one instrument, one
--      session, until one expiry. MANY may be live at once — the seat is plural, and
--      that plurality IS the competition.
--   3. `MandatePerformance` — THE ACCOUNTING. At the close the venue scores every
--      mandate against the batch that actually printed: what it was shown, what it
--      therefore owed, what it delivered, whether the print was inside its band, and
--      whether it met the commitment. The obligation is checkable AFTER THE FACT, by
--      the auditor, ON THE LEDGER, against a `SettlementBatch` the auditor already
--      holds. An obligation nobody can verify is a press release.
--
-- THE PROPERTY THAT MAKES THE WHOLE THING HANG TOGETHER: YOU MUST COMMIT BEFORE YOU
-- CAN SEE. `ClosingAuction.PublishImbalance` now demands a live mandate. There is no
-- path to the imbalance that does not run through an accepted obligation, so nobody
-- can read the residual and then decide whether to be a liquidity provider about it.
-- The sequence is forced: accept the duty blind, then be shown the number.
--
-- AND THERE IS NO RESIGNATION CHOICE. DELIBERATELY. A provider cannot hand the
-- mandate back when it sees an imbalance it dislikes — that would recreate the exact
-- optionality the mandate exists to remove, and it is the same rule the order book
-- already runs (`WithdrawOrder` is barred once the window seals: you cannot see the
-- cross coming and pull out of it). The mandate ends two ways only: it EXPIRES at
-- `expiresAt`, or it is REVOKED for failing (`RecordPerformance`). Both are bounded,
-- so nobody is trapped; neither is an escape hatch on the day.
--
-- THE CONSEQUENCE, KEPT HONEST. A provider that fails its commitment has its mandate
-- REVOKED — `RecordPerformance` simply does not re-create it — and is BARRED from
-- re-accepting these terms for this session, which is enforced by `AcceptTerms`
-- rather than by a rulebook. What this module deliberately does NOT model is a cash
-- penalty, a bond, or a fine. We do not hold provider collateral, so a monetary
-- penalty would be a number we could write down and never collect, and this project
-- does not ship things it cannot enforce. Losing the seat for the session — the thing
-- the venue actually controls — is the whole remedy, and it is the honest one.
-- ===========================================================================

module LiquidityMandate where

import Settlement (SettlementBatch)
import DA.List (dedup)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE BAND — how far from the anchor a provider promises to stand.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Same construction as the auction's own price collar (`MarketOnClose.collarBand`)
-- and the same idea NYSE Rule 7.35B(g)(2) uses to fence the DMM: the privileged
-- party's commitment is expressed relative to a number the venue PUBLISHED, so it
-- can be checked by anyone holding the anchor. Basis points, because that is the
-- unit an obligation of this kind is quoted in on every desk.
--
-- Deliberately NOT reusing `collarBand`: the collar is the venue's hard bound on the
-- PRINT, and the mandate band is the provider's own promise about WHERE IT WILL
-- STAND. They are different numbers with different owners, and a provider that
-- promised 200bps should not have its duty silently widened because the venue
-- widened its collar. One concept, one field, one owner.
bandOf : Decimal -> Int -> Decimal
bandOf anchor bps = anchor * intToDecimal bps / 10000.0

-- withinMandateBand — did the auction print somewhere this provider undertook to
-- trade? Inclusive at both edges, exactly as the collar is.
--
-- WHY THIS MATTERS AT SCORING TIME. The mandate says "up to `commitmentSize` WITHIN
-- `maxBandBps` of the anchor". If the book printed OUTSIDE that band, the provider
-- never promised to be there and its obligation is discharged — the venue does not
-- get to move the price away from the promise and then score the provider against it.
-- That is the mirror image of the Rule 7.35B(g)(2) fence: the privileged party is
-- bounded by the published number, and so is the venue.
withinMandateBand : Decimal -> Int -> Decimal -> Bool
withinMandateBand anchor bps price = abs (price - anchor) <= bandOf anchor bps

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- mandateIsLive / mandateCoversBook — THE DISCLOSURE GATE, as ONE definition.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `ClosingAuction.PublishImbalance` calls both of these and discloses nothing if
-- either is False. They live here, as pure functions over the mandate record, for
-- the same reason `eligibleAt` lives at the top of MarketOnClose: three places must
-- agree about what a live mandate is (the disclosure gate, the scoring choice, and
-- anything a future venue adds), and the only way to guarantee they agree is to give
-- them one function to agree with.
mandateIsLive : Time -> LiquidityMandate -> Bool
mandateIsLive now m = now < m.expiresAt

-- A mandate is scoped to exactly one book, identified by the same four fields every
-- other check in MarketOnClose identifies a book by: venue, instrument, cash leg,
-- session. A mandate for the cETH close buys you nothing at the AAPL close.
mandateCoversBook : LiquidityMandate -> Party -> Text -> Text -> Text -> Bool
mandateCoversBook m op inst cash sess =
  m.operator == op && m.instrumentId == inst
    && m.cashInstrument == cash && m.session == sess

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- MandateTerms — THE OPEN OFFER. The seat, posted where everyone can take it.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- This is the contestability half of the design, and every field on it is chosen to
-- keep entry cheap.
--
-- WHO MAY ACCEPT: everyone in `eligible`, which is the venue's REGISTERED
-- PARTICIPANT LIST — the same roster that lets you lodge a sealed order in the first
-- place. There is no separate application, no capital test, no membership class and
-- NO FEE ANYWHERE ON THIS CONTRACT. If you are allowed to trade the close, you are
-- allowed to bid for the right to see its residual, on exactly the terms everyone
-- else is offered. `PublishImbalance` re-checks participation at disclosure time, so
-- the roster and the seat cannot drift apart.
--
-- HOW MANY MAY ACCEPT: as many as want to. `accepted` grows; it is a record, not a
-- cap. Two, five or every participant may hold live mandates over the same book at
-- the same time and all of them are shown the same number. A single obligated
-- provider is a monopolist with a duty; five obligated providers compete to absorb
-- the imbalance, and the competition — not the paperwork — is what disciplines the
-- price they absorb it at. This is the direct implementation of the Hu & Murphy
-- remedy, and it is why `liquidityProvider : Optional Party` (ONE party, one slot)
-- could never have been fixed in place.
--
-- WHAT IT COSTS THE VENUE: the venue must publish terms it is willing to have taken
-- up by anybody on its roster, including a competitor it would not have chosen. That
-- is the point. A seat the venue can quietly award is a seat the venue can quietly
-- award to itself.
--
-- CONTENTION, STATED PLAINLY (the same trade-off `SubmitOrder` makes). `AcceptTerms`
-- is CONSUMING, because `accepted` and `barred` are ledger facts that have to move
-- with the acceptance, in the same transaction. So two providers accepting at the
-- same instant contend on this one contract and one of them retries. A mandate is
-- taken up once per session, not once per millisecond; paying in contention to have
-- the roster of obligated providers be provable is the right way round.
template MandateTerms
  with
    operator       : Party    -- the venue publishing the offer
    auditor        : Party    -- compliance observer of the mandate process
    instrumentId   : Text     -- the book these terms are for, e.g. "DEMO:AAPL"
    cashInstrument : Text     -- the cash leg of that book, e.g. "USDC"
    session        : Text     -- "Open" | "Close"
    -- THE ANCHOR THE BAND IS MEASURED FROM. Must equal the auction's published
    -- `referencePrice` — `PublishImbalance` asserts it — so a provider's promise is
    -- pinned to the same number the collar is centred on and the same number the
    -- imbalance is quoted against. One anchor, three uses, no drift.
    anchorPrice    : Decimal
    commitmentSize : Decimal  -- units of imbalance a provider undertakes to absorb
    maxBandBps     : Int      -- how far from the anchor it undertakes to stand
    expiresAt      : Time     -- terms close here, and so does every mandate struck off them
    eligible       : [Party]  -- the venue's registered participants: the OPEN offer
    accepted       : [Party]  -- who currently holds a live mandate off these terms
    -- BARRED — providers that FAILED a commitment this session. The only way onto
    -- this list is `BarProvider`, which demands an on-ledger `MandatePerformance`
    -- recording the failure: the venue cannot bar a competitor it simply dislikes.
    barred         : [Party]
  where
    signatory operator
    -- EVERY eligible participant observes the offer, plus the auditor. An open offer
    -- nobody can see is not an open offer; this observer line is the contestability,
    -- expressed as visibility.
    observer (auditor :: eligible)

    ensure commitmentSize > 0.0
        && maxBandBps > 0
        && anchorPrice > 0.0
        && (session == "Open" || session == "Close")
        && not (null eligible)
        && length (dedup eligible) == length eligible
        && length (dedup accepted) == length accepted
        && length (dedup barred)   == length barred

    -- ACCEPT TERMS — any eligible participant takes up the offer and becomes an
    -- obligated provider. THIS IS THE CONTESTABLE ENTRY POINT, and note who controls
    -- it: the PROVIDER, not the venue. The venue posts terms; it does not choose who
    -- takes them. The operator's authority to co-sign the resulting mandate arrives
    -- because the operator signs THESE TERMS — which is exactly the consent structure
    -- you want, because it means the venue consented once, publicly, to whoever shows
    -- up, rather than privately, to one name.
    --
    -- ACCEPTANCE IS BLIND, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN. Nothing about the resting book is
    -- visible from here, and `PublishImbalance` will not disclose an imbalance to a
    -- party without a mandate — so a participant must take on the duty BEFORE it can
    -- see the number the duty is about. There is no read-then-decide.
    choice AcceptTerms : (ContractId MandateTerms, ContractId LiquidityMandate)
      with
        provider : Party    -- the participant taking up the seat (the controller)
      controller provider
      do
        now <- getTime
        assertMsg "these mandate terms have expired"                  (now < expiresAt)
        assertMsg "only a registered participant of this venue may take up a mandate"
          (provider `elem` eligible)
        -- THE CONSEQUENCE, ENFORCED HERE. A provider barred for failing a commitment
        -- this session cannot simply take the seat again — this is the assert that
        -- makes revocation mean something.
        assertMsg "provider is barred from these terms for this session"
          (provider `notElem` barred)
        assertMsg "provider already holds a live mandate off these terms"
          (provider `notElem` accepted)
        mandateCid <- create LiquidityMandate with
          operator; auditor; provider
          instrumentId; cashInstrument; session
          anchorPrice; commitmentSize; maxBandBps; expiresAt
          acceptedAt = now
          -- Nothing has been shown yet, so nothing is owed yet. See `NoteDisclosure`.
          shownSide = "Flat"; peakShownQty = 0.0
          lastShownAt = None; disclosuresSeen = 0
        termsCid <- create this with accepted = provider :: accepted
        pure (termsCid, mandateCid)

    -- BAR PROVIDER — the consequence half, and it is EVIDENCE-GATED.
    -- ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- The venue cannot bar whoever it likes. It must present a `MandatePerformance`
    -- for THIS book naming THIS provider and recording `met = False` — a contract
    -- that only `RecordPerformance` produces, from the batch that actually printed.
    -- So the bar is not an administrative act; it is a derived consequence of a
    -- recorded failure that the provider and the auditor can both read.
    --
    -- Exercised automatically inside `LiquidityMandate.RecordPerformance` when a
    -- provider misses, so revocation and the bar land in ONE transaction and cannot
    -- come apart. It is exposed as its own choice so the venue can also bar on a
    -- performance recorded earlier in the session.
    choice BarProvider : ContractId MandateTerms
      with
        provider       : Party
        performanceCid : ContractId MandatePerformance   -- the on-ledger failure
      controller operator
      do
        perf <- fetch performanceCid
        assertMsg "performance record is from a different venue"      (perf.operator == operator)
        assertMsg "performance record is for a different instrument"  (perf.instrumentId == instrumentId)
        assertMsg "performance record is for a different cash leg"    (perf.cashInstrument == cashInstrument)
        assertMsg "performance record is for a different session"     (perf.session == session)
        assertMsg "performance record names a different provider"     (perf.provider == provider)
        assertMsg "a provider that MET its commitment cannot be barred" (not perf.met)
        create this with
          barred   = provider :: filter (/= provider) barred
          accepted = filter (/= provider) accepted

    -- ADMIT PARTICIPANT — keep the offer open to a participant registered after the
    -- terms were posted. Entry stays cheap over the life of the session rather than
    -- only at the instant the venue happened to publish.
    choice AdmitParticipant : ContractId MandateTerms
      with
        participant : Party
      controller operator
      do
        assertMsg "participant is already eligible" (participant `notElem` eligible)
        create this with eligible = participant :: eligible

    -- WITHDRAW TERMS — the venue takes the offer down (the session is over, or the
    -- terms are being re-posted). It does NOT touch mandates already struck: an
    -- obligation the venue can cancel by withdrawing its own offer is not an
    -- obligation. Live mandates run to their own `expiresAt`.
    choice WithdrawTerms : ()
      controller operator
      do pure ()

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- LiquidityMandate — THE OBLIGATION. Operator and provider, both signing.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHY BOTH SIGN, AND WHY THAT IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE FROM A FIELD. The old design
-- put a party's name in `ClosingAuction.liquidityProvider` — a value the venue wrote
-- unilaterally into its own contract. The named party never agreed to anything, was
-- never asked, and owed nothing. THIS contract cannot exist unless the provider's
-- authority was present when it was created, which happens in exactly one place:
-- `MandateTerms.AcceptTerms`, controlled by the provider. The obligation is therefore
-- consented to in the strict ledger sense, and its existence is the proof of consent.
--
-- WHAT IT BINDS THE PROVIDER TO: absorb up to `commitmentSize` units of the disclosed
-- imbalance, on the CONTRA side (a buy-heavy book needs the provider to SELL), at a
-- print within `maxBandBps` of `anchorPrice`, for this instrument, this cash leg and
-- this session, until `expiresAt`.
--
-- WHAT IT BUYS THE PROVIDER: sight of the net imbalance, and nothing else. Not the
-- individual orders, not the traders, not the indicative price — `PublishImbalance`
-- copies none of that onto the disclosure. The mandate buys the aggregate and the
-- aggregate only, which is the same shape of privilege the DMM has (more than the
-- tape, far less than the book) with the same shape of duty attached.
--
-- THE RUNNING RECORD (`shownSide` / `peakShownQty` / `lastShownAt` /
-- `disclosuresSeen`). A duty has to be sized by something, and the honest something is
-- WHAT THIS PROVIDER WAS ACTUALLY SHOWN. Every `PublishImbalance` stamps the number
-- onto the mandate through `NoteDisclosure`, so the obligation the venue scores at the
-- close is the obligation the ledger recorded during the session — not a figure
-- produced afterwards, and not one the provider can claim never to have received.
template LiquidityMandate
  with
    operator        : Party     -- the venue
    auditor         : Party     -- compliance observer of the obligation
    provider        : Party     -- the obligated liquidity provider
    instrumentId    : Text
    cashInstrument  : Text
    session         : Text      -- "Open" | "Close"
    anchorPrice     : Decimal   -- must equal the auction's published referencePrice
    commitmentSize  : Decimal   -- units of imbalance this provider will absorb
    maxBandBps      : Int       -- how far from the anchor it will stand
    expiresAt       : Time      -- the duty ends here; there is no earlier exit
    acceptedAt      : Time      -- when the provider took the seat
    -- THE RUNNING DISCLOSURE RECORD — written ONLY by `NoteDisclosure`, which is
    -- called from `ClosingAuction.PublishImbalance`.
    shownSide       : Text      -- "Buy" | "Sell" | "Flat": side of the PEAK imbalance shown
    peakShownQty    : Decimal   -- the LARGEST imbalance magnitude this mandate was shown
    lastShownAt     : Optional Time  -- None = never shown anything
    disclosuresSeen : Int       -- how many times the venue opened the book to it
  where
    signatory operator, provider
    observer auditor

    ensure commitmentSize > 0.0
        && maxBandBps > 0
        && anchorPrice > 0.0
        && peakShownQty >= 0.0
        && disclosuresSeen >= 0
        && expiresAt > acceptedAt
        && (session == "Open" || session == "Close")
        && (shownSide == "Buy" || shownSide == "Sell" || shownSide == "Flat")

    -- NOTE DISCLOSURE — stamp what the provider was just shown onto its own mandate.
    -- ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- Called from `ClosingAuction.PublishImbalance` in the same transaction that
    -- creates the `ImbalanceDisclosure`, so the privilege and the record of the
    -- privilege are inseparable: the provider cannot be shown a number without the
    -- ledger writing down that it was shown it.
    --
    -- WHY THE PEAK AND NOT THE LATEST — the one genuinely debatable choice in this
    -- module, so here is the argument. Take "the latest": a provider shown a buy
    -- imbalance of 8 sells 8 into it, the venue republishes, the book is Flat, and the
    -- provider's obligation is now ZERO — its own performance erased the duty it
    -- performed. Worse, so does anything else that flattens the book, so a venue
    -- republishing a small final number lets any provider off. The obligation
    -- evaporates exactly when it is satisfied, which makes it unmeasurable.
    -- Take "the peak": you cannot un-see what you were shown. A provider shown 8 owes
    -- min(commitment, 8) for the session, whoever else turns up.
    --   THE HONEST COST OF THE PEAK RULE, said out loud: a provider that stands aside
    --   while OTHER traders fill the book is recorded as not having delivered, even
    --   though the close did not need it. We take that, for two reasons. First, this
    --   is a BACKSTOP duty, not a best-efforts one — NYSE Rule 104(e) makes the DMM
    --   take the unpaired odd-lot remainder whether or not it feels the market needs
    --   it, and a backstop that appears only when it agrees the market is short is not
    --   a backstop. Second, standing in costs the provider almost nothing: it trades
    --   at the official uniform print, inside a band it chose, in a size it chose.
    --   The asymmetry between "filled at the close you helped make" and "the residual
    --   went unfilled because the one party who could see it did nothing" is the whole
    --   reason the mandate exists.
    --
    -- WHAT THIS DOES NOT PREVENT, STATED PLAINLY. The operator signs this contract, so
    -- it can exercise this choice DIRECTLY and stamp a number no book ever produced —
    -- inflating a provider's duty to manufacture a failure. The ledger does not stop
    -- that; what it does is make it VISIBLE TO THE PARTY IT INJURES. The provider
    -- co-signs this mandate and sees every version of it, holds its own
    -- `ImbalanceDisclosure` contracts to compare against, observes its
    -- `MandatePerformance`, and observes the `MandateTerms` its bar is written onto.
    -- A venue that fakes an obligation is doing it in front of the counterparty and
    -- the auditor. That is the same standard the rest of this codebase holds itself
    -- to (see `VenueCancel`: possible, visible, and self-defeating), and it is stated
    -- here rather than papered over.
    choice NoteDisclosure : ContractId LiquidityMandate
      with
        netSide     : Text      -- side the BOOK is heavy on: "Buy" | "Sell" | "Flat"
        netQuantity : Decimal   -- magnitude of that imbalance (>= 0)
      controller operator
      do
        now <- getTime
        assertMsg "a disclosed side must be Buy, Sell or Flat"
          (netSide == "Buy" || netSide == "Sell" || netSide == "Flat")
        assertMsg "a disclosed imbalance cannot be negative" (netQuantity >= 0.0)
        assertMsg "cannot disclose against an expired mandate" (mandateIsLive now this)
        -- Monotone in the magnitude: a bigger imbalance replaces the peak AND the side
        -- it points at (the side is only meaningful attached to the number it came
        -- with). A smaller one leaves the duty where it was and only bumps the counter.
        let bigger = netQuantity > peakShownQty
        create this with
          peakShownQty    = if bigger then netQuantity else peakShownQty
          shownSide       = if bigger then netSide     else shownSide
          lastShownAt     = Some now
          disclosuresSeen = disclosuresSeen + 1

    -- RECORD PERFORMANCE — the accountability half. Score the mandate against the
    -- batch that actually printed, and revoke it if it missed.
    -- ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    -- CONSUMING, AND THE CONSUMPTION IS THE MECHANISM: a mandate that MET its
    -- commitment is RE-CREATED (the provider keeps the seat until `expiresAt`); a
    -- mandate that FAILED is not re-created, and that absence IS the revocation. The
    -- bar follows in the same transaction. There is no state in which a failed
    -- provider is revoked but not barred, or barred but still holding a live mandate —
    -- the two facts move together or the transaction rolls back.
    --
    -- WHAT IS MEASURED, AND WHERE EVERY NUMBER COMES FROM:
    --   owed      = min commitmentSize peakShownQty, and ZERO if the print landed
    --               outside the band the provider undertook to stand in. Both inputs
    --               are ledger facts written before the close: `commitmentSize` at
    --               acceptance, `peakShownQty` by the disclosures.
    --   delivered = this provider's CONTRA-side fills in the batch. A buy-heavy book
    --               (`shownSide == "Buy"`) needed selling, so the provider's sells
    --               count — in `RunClose` a sell leg records `seller = trader`. A
    --               sell-heavy book needed buying, so its buys count (`buyer =
    --               trader`). Nothing else counts: filling the SAME side as the
    --               imbalance makes the imbalance worse and is not performance.
    --   met       = delivered >= owed.
    -- Every one of those comes off `SettlementBatch`, which the AUDITOR ALREADY
    -- OBSERVES, and the performance record carries the batch's contract id — so the
    -- auditor can recompute the arithmetic independently rather than take the venue's
    -- word for it. That is what "recorded on-ledger" has to mean to be worth anything.
    choice RecordPerformance : MandateOutcome
      with
        batchCid : ContractId SettlementBatch   -- the close that actually printed
        termsCid : ContractId MandateTerms      -- the offer this mandate was struck off
      controller operator
      do
        now <- getTime
        b <- fetch batchCid
        assertMsg "settlement batch is from a different venue"      (b.operator == operator)
        assertMsg "settlement batch is for a different instrument"  (b.instrumentId == instrumentId)
        t <- fetch termsCid
        assertMsg "terms are from a different venue"                (t.operator == operator)
        assertMsg "terms are for a different instrument"            (t.instrumentId == instrumentId)
        assertMsg "terms are for a different cash leg"              (t.cashInstrument == cashInstrument)
        assertMsg "terms are for a different session"               (t.session == session)

        let printed    = b.closingPrice
            -- THE BAND FENCE, IN THE PROVIDER'S FAVOUR. A print outside the band is a
            -- price this provider never undertook to stand at, so it owes nothing
            -- there. The venue cannot walk the close away from the promise and then
            -- score the provider against the promise.
            inBand     = withinMandateBand anchorPrice maxBandBps printed
            owed       = if inBand then min commitmentSize peakShownQty else 0.0
            -- CONTRA-SIDE FILLS ONLY. See the comment above for why the sides map
            -- this way round.
            delivered  = case shownSide of
              "Buy"  -> sum [f.quantity | f <- b.fills, f.seller == provider]
              "Sell" -> sum [f.quantity | f <- b.fills, f.buyer  == provider]
              _      -> 0.0
            met        = delivered >= owed

        perf <- create MandatePerformance with
          operator; auditor; provider
          instrumentId; cashInstrument; session
          anchorPrice; printedPrice = printed; maxBandBps; withinBand = inBand
          shownSide; shownQty = peakShownQty; disclosuresSeen
          committed = commitmentSize; owed; delivered; met
          batchRef = batchCid
          recordedAt = now

        -- MET → the mandate survives to its own expiry, so a provider that performs
        -- keeps the seat for the rest of the session without re-accepting anything.
        -- FAILED → it is simply not re-created. That is the revocation.
        survivor <- if met
          then do c <- create this; pure (Some c)
          else pure None

        -- FAILED → the bar, in the SAME transaction, justified by the record created
        -- three lines above. `BarProvider` re-checks the book and `met = False` for
        -- itself; it does not take this choice's word for it.
        termsAfter <- if met
          then pure termsCid
          else exercise termsCid BarProvider with provider; performanceCid = perf

        pure MandateOutcome with
          performance = perf
          mandate     = survivor
          terms       = termsAfter

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- MandateOutcome — what scoring a mandate leaves behind.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `mandate = None` IS the revocation — there is no surviving obligation contract,
-- and the provider will fail `AcceptTerms` on the returned `terms` because it is now
-- on the barred list. Returning all three cids together means a caller cannot act on
-- half the outcome.
data MandateOutcome = MandateOutcome
  with
    performance : ContractId MandatePerformance
    mandate     : Optional (ContractId LiquidityMandate)  -- None = REVOKED
    terms       : ContractId MandateTerms                 -- barred, if it failed
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- MandatePerformance — committed vs delivered, on the ledger, for the auditor.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE OTHER HALF OF THE BARGAIN. An obligation that is never scored is a slogan, and
-- a scoring nobody outside the venue can see is a slogan with a spreadsheet. This
-- contract is the venue's signed statement of what one provider was shown, what it
-- therefore owed, and what it actually delivered into the print — kept as an
-- immutable record after the mandate itself is gone.
--
-- WHO SIGNS AND WHY IT IS NOT THE PROVIDER. Signatory: the operator alone. If the
-- provider co-signed, a provider could veto its own failing scorecard simply by not
-- signing, and the consequence in `BarProvider` would be unreachable — the mechanism
-- would work only against providers who agreed to be punished. So the venue writes
-- the record and the provider OBSERVES it, alongside the auditor. The check on the
-- venue is not a signature, it is `batchRef`: every number here is recomputable from
-- a `SettlementBatch` the auditor already holds and the mandate the provider
-- co-signed. A false record is arithmetic that does not reconcile, in front of the
-- two parties best placed to notice.
template MandatePerformance
  with
    operator        : Party
    auditor         : Party
    provider        : Party
    instrumentId    : Text
    cashInstrument  : Text
    session         : Text
    anchorPrice     : Decimal   -- the published anchor the band was measured from
    printedPrice    : Decimal   -- what the close actually printed
    maxBandBps      : Int       -- the band the provider undertook to stand in
    withinBand      : Bool      -- did the print land inside it? (False ⇒ owed 0)
    shownSide       : Text      -- side of the peak imbalance disclosed to it
    shownQty        : Decimal   -- the peak imbalance disclosed to it
    disclosuresSeen : Int       -- how many times it was shown the book
    committed       : Decimal   -- commitmentSize on the mandate
    owed            : Decimal   -- min committed shownQty, or 0 outside the band
    delivered       : Decimal   -- contra-side units it actually filled in the batch
    met             : Bool      -- delivered >= owed
    batchRef        : ContractId SettlementBatch  -- recompute the numbers yourself
    recordedAt      : Time
  where
    signatory operator
    -- The provider sees its own scorecard; the auditor sees every scorecard. Nobody
    -- else does — a provider's performance is not published to its competitors, for
    -- the same reason the book is not.
    observer auditor, provider

    ensure committed > 0.0
        && owed >= 0.0
        && delivered >= 0.0
        && shownQty >= 0.0
        && anchorPrice > 0.0
        && printedPrice > 0.0
        && maxBandBps > 0
        && met == (delivered >= owed)     -- the verdict must match its own numbers
        && (shownSide == "Buy" || shownSide == "Sell" || shownSide == "Flat")
